# /etc/pam.d/dms-fido2 — auth stack for the DankMaterialShell lock screen.
#
# Same stack as bootstrap/pam/hyprlock-fido2, for the same reasons, wired into
# DMS instead of hyprlock. Kept as a SEPARATE file rather than pointing DMS at
# hyprlock-fido2 so the two lock screens can diverge later without one silently
# changing the other; the contents are deliberately identical today.
#
# WHY DMS USES THIS AT ALL — DMS is in "custom primary PAM" mode:
#
#     lockPamPath      = /etc/pam.d/dms-fido2
#     lockPamInlineU2f = true
#
# DMS's own security-key handling (enableU2f / u2fMode) is left OFF. In its
# native mode the key is a separate factor you start on demand with the passkey
# button, which costs a keypress on every unlock; with an inline pam_u2f in the
# primary stack, DMS sees `lockPamInlineU2f` and suppresses its own factor UI
# (Pam.qml: `u2fSuppressedByPrimaryPam`), so the key is armed the instant the
# screen locks and a bare touch unlocks — the behaviour hyprlock gives today.
#
# ⚠️ FAILURE MODE THAT IS SILENT AND MUST BE VERIFIED. DMS only uses this file
# if it can actually READ it (Pam.qml: `customPamActive = lockPamPath !== "" &&
# customPamWatcher.loaded`). If the file is missing or not world-readable, DMS
# degrades with no error shown. So a missing file does not lock you out, it
# degrades you to the old broken behaviour. Verify with `mise run
# bootstrap:pam-dms`, which asserts the file is in place and readable.
#
# WHAT IT DEGRADES *TO*, on dms 1.5.3 — not `login`, as this comment said until
# 2026-09-02. When `customPamActive` is false and there is no managed
# /etc/pam.d/dankshell, DMS runs `dms auth resolve-lock` and writes its own
# fallback stack to ~/.local/state/DankMaterialShell/pam/dankshell (Pam.qml:
# `ensureUserPamConfig`, upstream #2789), then authenticates against that. The
# generated file is a copy of system-auth, so it carries BOTH things this stack
# exists to avoid — `pam_sss.so forward_pass` and `pam_faildelay delay=2000000`
# — and no pam_u2f at all. The destination changed; the trap did not.
#
# It is visible in the journal, which is the only place it shows up:
#
#     dms[…]: Starting pam session for user "jo" with config "dankshell"
#             in dir "/home/jo/.local/state/DankMaterialShell/pam"
#
# versus a healthy session, which names this file's basename and its directory:
#
#     dms[…]: Starting pam session for user "jo" with config "dms-fido2"
#             in dir "/etc/pam.d"
#
# ⚠️⚠️ INSTALLING THIS FILE INTO A RUNNING SESSION IS NOT ENOUGH. `customPamWatcher`
# (Pam.qml) is a FileView with NO `watchChanges`, and the per-lock reset path
# reloads only `u2fConfigWatcher` — the comment there ("FileView cannot watch a
# path that does not exist yet; re-read so a dedicated service created after
# startup is used on the next lock") describes this watcher's problem exactly but
# does not cover it. So `customPamActive` is decided ONCE, when dms.service
# starts, and a file installed afterwards is never noticed:
#
#     mise run bootstrap:pam-dms        # says "Installed / matches"
#     …and the lock screen still uses the dankshell fallback, all session long.
#
# The task cannot detect this — it checks the filesystem, and the filesystem is
# fine. After installing, either log out and back in, or:
#
#     systemctl --user restart dms.service
#
# On a bootstrapped machine this never bites, because the file is in place long
# before the first DMS login. It bites exactly once, on the machine where you
# just discovered the file was missing — i.e. the moment you most believe you
# have fixed it. (Diagnosed 2026-09-02 on the Fedora 44 machine.)
#
# WHY NOT the shipped `auth include login`: `login` pulls in system-auth, whose
# authselect **sssd** profile appends, after pam_unix:
#
#     auth  sufficient  pam_sss.so forward_pass
#
# There is no SSSD domain on this machine, so that module only ever prompts for
# the password a second time and fails. That is the "the FIDO stick is not active
# until I hit Enter three times" symptom (diagnosed 2026-08-06):
#
#   1. locking starts a PAM transaction; pam_u2f arms the key immediately
#   2. ~31s later the CTAP device timeout lapses and the u2f leg is spent; PAM
#      falls through to pam_unix's "Password:" — the key is now dead, but the
#      lock screen still says "touch key"
#   3. returning minutes later, Enter #1 fails pam_unix, Enter #2 fails pam_sss,
#      pam_deny ends the transaction, and only then does a fresh transaction
#      re-arm the key — so the touch is the third interaction
#
# Dropping pam_sss makes one Enter enough. This also drops
# `pam_faildelay delay=2000000` — a 2s penalty per failed attempt that a lock
# screen has no use for — plus pam_env/pam_nologin/pam_securetty/pam_selinux,
# none of which mean anything for unlocking an existing session.
#
# The ~31s window itself is NOT fixable here: it is a CTAP timeout in the
# authenticator, and pam_u2f exposes no timeout option. NB hyprlock re-arms the
# key by starting a fresh transaction (Pam.cpp:86-105); DMS has its own retry
# timers (Pam.qml: errorRetry / u2fErrorRetry / passwdActiveTimeout) but whether
# they re-arm on the same schedule is UNVERIFIED — if a stale lock screen ever
# needs an extra Enter before the key responds, that is this window, not a
# misconfiguration.
#
# `nodelay` on pam_unix, added 2026-08-20. Dropping pam_faildelay (above) removed
# only the *stack's* penalty; pam_unix requests a delay-on-failure of its own —
# "of the order of two seconds" per pam_unix(8) — and that one survived. It is
# visible in every journal pair: the `authentication failure` line and hyprlock's
# `auth: Authentication failed` sit 2-3s apart, and hyprlock only starts the next
# transaction (which re-arms the key) after that. A touch during those 2s lands on
# an unarmed key and is lost, which is the likeliest reason ~25% of unlocks in the
# 8-day sample cost two to four submissions rather than one. A lock screen has no
# use for a brute-force delay: there is no pam_faillock anywhere in this system's
# stack, and an attacker at the lock screen is not rate-limited by 2s anyway.
#
# ORDER: pam_u2f first, so a bare touch unlocks and the key is armed the instant
# the screen locks. pam_unix is `required` rather than `sufficient` so this stack
# cannot silently pass, and carries no `nullok` — an empty submission must fail
# (that failure is what re-arms the key).
#
# Nothing is given up relative to the login stack: there is no pam_faillock
# anywhere in this system's PAM config. The `account` line is defensive, for a
# PAM caller that runs pam_acct_mgmt.
#
# pam_u2f's own options live in /etc/security/pam_u2f.conf (userverification = 1,
# i.e. the on-key fingerprint sensor of the Kensington VeriMark Guard must verify
# the user, not merely that someone is present); see bootstrap/pam/pam_u2f.conf.
# Options on a module line would override that file, so deliberately none are set
# here beyond `cue`. The enrolled credential is ~/.config/Yubico/u2f_keys, which
# is pam_u2f's default authfile — so no `authfile=` is needed either.
#
# Install via:  mise run bootstrap:pam-dms
# Selected by:  lockPamPath in ~/.config/DankMaterialShell/settings.json
# See ~/Documents/docs/hyprland-dms-quickshell-session.md

auth        sufficient  pam_u2f.so cue
auth        required    pam_unix.so nodelay

account     required    pam_unix.so
